vms-4ed (component C2 of vms-5f5): OVMX-private EVAX_R_OVMX_GPDISP relocation.

[OVMX] Adds the OVMX GP-displacement relocation to the alpha-dec-vms binutils so
gas can mark a callee's ldah/lda GP-establish prologue pair for the OVMX linker
to patch with -K (K = the enclosing procedure's PDSC offset within its module
linkage section).  EVAX/VSI publishes NO GP-displacement encoding (OSF/Alpha uses
ldgp/GPDISP; the EVAX ETIR command set has none), so this is an OVMX design
choice, LABELED OVMX throughout and never presented as VMS-authentic
(CLAUDE.md Rule 8).  See docs/design-alpha-per-image-gp.md 2.1/2.2.

Wire form (OVMX): a new OVMX-private ETIR command ETIR__C_OVMX_GPDISP = 0xEF01
(OVMX-private ETIR range 0xEF00-0xEFFF, OUTSIDE VSI's opcode space so a real VSI
object can never collide) with the standard cmd[2]+length[2] header (length incl.
header) and operand, all little-endian:

    [u32 lkidx_or_0][u32 psect][u64 ldah_vaddr][u32 lda_delta][u8 namelen][name]

name = the enclosing procedure symbol whose PDSC offset K the OVMX linker looks
up; -K is signed-split (ldah hi with the +0x8000 carry, lda lo) into the pair.

New assembler directive `.ovmx_gpdisp <procsym>` is placed immediately after the
ldah/lda pair; it emits BFD_RELOC_ALPHA_OVMX_GPDISP (internal ALPHA_R_OVMX_GPDISP,
howto size 0 so the two instruction words stay ordinary content), which
_bfd_vms_write_etir serializes as the command above.  The OVMX linker
(src/vmslink/evax_read.c recognizer + link.c apply) resolves it; bfd never
applies it (reloc_nil).

Files:
  include/vms/etir.h       define ETIR__C_OVMX_GPDISP (OVMX-private range)
  bfd/bfd-in2.h            new BFD_RELOC_ALPHA_OVMX_GPDISP code
  bfd/reloc.c              matching ENUMX/ENUMDOC (enum consistency)
  bfd/vms-alpha.c          ALPHA_R_OVMX_GPDISP index + howto + type-lookup + ETIR emit
  gas/config/tc-alpha.c    .ovmx_gpdisp directive + pseudo-op + md_apply_fix passthrough

Clean-room (Rule 8): derived only from the public GPL binutils vms-alpha layout
and public OpenVMS docs; no VSI/HPE source or binary was consulted.
